# AGENTS.md

{{synopsis}}. The owner reviews every change.
Write code that {{reader}} can read. Setup is in `README.md`.

## Checks

`scripts/check` runs every gate: the lockfile, a file-type check, oxfmt, oxlint with type
information, a search for switched-off checks, the type check, knip for unused code, and the
vitest tests. The git hook and CI run it too.

- Work in small steps and keep `scripts/check` green after each one.
- Done means `scripts/check` is green. Show the command and the end of its output.
- When a check stays red after two honest attempts, stop and report what you tried.
- Fix the cause of a red check. Gate files change only with the owner's explicit approval:
  `scripts/check`, `.githooks/`, `.github/`, `.claude/`, `tsconfig.json`, `.oxlintrc.json`,
  `.oxfmtrc.json`, `knip.jsonc`, `vitest.config.ts`, `tests/setup.ts`, `pnpm-workspace.yaml`,
  `mise.toml`, `mise.lock`, `.gitignore`, `.gitattributes`, `devEngines` and `scripts` in
  `package.json`, and existing tests.

`scripts/check` starts itself through `mise exec`, so it works in a shell without mise. Other
commands need the same prefix:

```sh
mise exec -- pnpm exec vitest run -t "greets the world"         # one test by name
mise exec -- pnpm exec vitest run tests/{{package}}.test.ts     # one test file
mise exec -- pnpm fmt                                           # format
mise exec -- pnpm lint --fix                                    # fix what lint can fix
mise exec -- pnpm typecheck                                     # type check only
```

After a branch switch or a change to `package.json`, `pnpm exec` stops with "Run pnpm install".
Run `mise exec -- pnpm install` then. Use pnpm only: npm refuses to run here.

Sandboxed agents: pnpm writes to its store and cache (`~/Library/pnpm`, `~/Library/Caches/pnpm`
on macOS; `~/.local/share/pnpm`, `~/.cache/pnpm` on Linux), and mise to `~/.local/share/mise`.
`pnpm install`, `pnpm add` and `gh` need the network. Without these, `scripts/check` fails for
reasons outside the code.

Language server: `node_modules/.bin/tsc --lsp --stdio` (TypeScript 7). The older
`typescript-language-server` needs `tsserver`, which TypeScript 7 does not ship.

## TypeScript

Read `CODING_STANDARDS.md` before writing or reviewing TypeScript.

The checks enforce these rules:

- Source files are `.ts` only: no `.js`, `.mts`, `.cts`, `.tsx`, and no hand-written `.d.ts`.
  No committed file under an ignored path such as `dist/`.
- Only `mise.toml` configures mise: no `mise.local.toml`, `.tool-versions` or `.config/mise/`.
- Relative imports name the `.ts` file (`./parse.ts`). Built-in modules use `node:` (`node:fs`).
- Only TypeScript that Node can strip: no `enum`, `namespace` or constructor parameter
  properties. Use a union of string literals and plain fields.
- No `any`, no `!`, no `as` that narrows a type. Data from outside is `unknown` until a type
  guard checks it.
- No `declare` (`declare function`, `declare global`): import what the code uses, or define it.
- A `switch` on a union handles every member. Compare with `=== undefined` or `=== ""`
  instead of relying on truthiness.
- Tests use fixed inputs: no clock and no `Math.random` or `crypto` random values, however the
  test reaches them (`tests/setup.ts` fails it; code under `src/` may read them), and no
  fast-check seed, `numRuns` or time limit other than in `tests/setup.ts`. Fake timers start at
  0. No skipped, conditional, expected-to-fail or retried tests. A missing snapshot fails; write
  it on purpose with `vitest run -u`.
- Every promise is awaited or handled with `.catch`; `void promise` does not count.
- No unused parameter or variable, also not one named `_name`. A parameter before a used one may
  stay unused (`(_, index) => index`).
- No empty block, such as `catch {}`.
- No comment that switches off a check (`oxlint-disable`, `@ts-expect-error`,
  `prettier-ignore` and the like).

## Layout

- Logic goes in the library under `src/`. `src/main.ts` only reads the command line, calls it
  and prints.
- `tests/` holds the vitest suite. Every `tests/**/*.test.ts` file runs.
- knip fails on an unused file, export or dependency. A new program needs a script in
  `package.json`, so knip sees it as an entry point.
- Node and pnpm versions live in `mise.toml` and in `devEngines` in `package.json`. Change
  both together.

## Writing

For chat replies, commits, PRs, code comments and docs. Many readers are not native English
speakers.

- Short sentences and plain words. Lead with the result. Then what the reader needs to act: what
  changed, what failed, what is left, what you did not verify.
- Say what a technical idea does before you name it.
- A chat reply fits one screen. Use a table for parallel facts and a numbered list for steps.
  Keep every risk and open question.
- Name files by repo-relative path.
- Docs state rules and pointers, never project status. Status goes stale.
- Commits and PRs describe the change only, with no tool or AI attribution.
- PR title: `type(scope): what changed`, at most 72 characters. CI checks it. PR body: fill
  `.github/pull_request_template.md`, one line per slot. One concern per PR, about 200 changed
  lines at most. Open it with `gh pr create --assignee @me`, so the owner is assigned. Stack
  bigger work as PRs whose base is the branch below. Squash merges make the PR title and body the
  commit on `main`.

## Review

Review each PR on its own. Before a stack merges, review it once as a whole for problems between
PRs only:

```sh
git diff $(git merge-base origin/main <top>)..<top>
```
